\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {6}{\ignorespaces Numerical illustration of the skew product with solenoid base. \textbf  {(A)} Orbit of a typical point under the solenoid map $T(\theta ,x,y)=(10\theta \bmod  1,\tfrac  {x}{10}+\cos (2\pi \theta ),\tfrac  {y}{10}+\sin (2\pi \theta ))$, plotted in $(\theta ,x,y)$--coordinates. The dense trajectory illustrates invertibility, hyperbolicity, and mixing of the base dynamics. \textbf  {(B)} Fiber dynamics: successive push--forwards of Lebesgue measure on the disk $B$ under cocycles generated by the base orbit. After a few steps the distribution collapses onto a thin one-dimensional ``spine,'' producing the characteristic porcupine geometry. \textbf  {(C)} Equivariance: comparison between the empirical invariant measure $\mu _a$ in a typical fiber (blue) and its push--forward $(f_a)_*\mu _a$ (orange). The almost perfect overlap confirms uniqueness and invariance of the fiberwise SRB--like measure. Together, the three panels illustrate the structural dichotomy of Theorem\nonbreakingspace \ref {thm1} and the statistical convergence asserted in Corollary\nonbreakingspace \ref {cor:unique_ergodic}.}}{50}{figure.6}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{pic44}{{6}{50}{Numerical illustration of the skew product with solenoid base. \textbf {(A)} Orbit of a typical point under the solenoid map $T(\theta ,x,y)=(10\theta \bmod 1,\tfrac {x}{10}+\cos (2\pi \theta ),\tfrac {y}{10}+\sin (2\pi \theta ))$, plotted in $(\theta ,x,y)$--coordinates. The dense trajectory illustrates invertibility, hyperbolicity, and mixing of the base dynamics. \textbf {(B)} Fiber dynamics: successive push--forwards of Lebesgue measure on the disk $B$ under cocycles generated by the base orbit. After a few steps the distribution collapses onto a thin one-dimensional ``spine,'' producing the characteristic porcupine geometry. \textbf {(C)} Equivariance: comparison between the empirical invariant measure $\mu _a$ in a typical fiber (blue) and its push--forward $(f_a)_*\mu _a$ (orange). The almost perfect overlap confirms uniqueness and invariance of the fiberwise SRB--like measure. Together, the three panels illustrate the structural dichotomy of Theorem~\ref {thm1} and the statistical convergence asserted in Corollary~\ref {cor:unique_ergodic}}{figure.6}{}}
